Good catch, and that is a real concern, but the node ID is part of the stamp, so two devices never produce an identical HLC. Within the same device, the next `tick` after `receive` has wall equal to the stored wall, so it increments and stays strictly greater than what we stored. Ordering against the remote stamp holds through wall first, then counter, then node.

I tried a variant first that kept `remote.counter + 1` for the remote-wall branch, and it kept failing the skew test, so I reverted it. Final version:

